bpftune (0.4.70) UNRELEASED; urgency=medium

  * restore the second-best fallback on the rate target.

    0.4.45 changed the swap target source from the composite metric
    (which had a second-best fallback predating the fork) to rate_ema
    (no fallback).  When a socket is already on the rate leader and
    that leader is failing for the socket, the chain reaches
    swap_tgt == s and does nothing.  Cookie 68810 (0.4.69): socket
    on vegas at 1.35 Mbps while vegas rate_ema was 81 (8.1 MB/s);
    no swap could fire on a socket 60x below its own algorithm's EMA.

    Fix: pass 3 tracks a second-best rate target by the same weighted
    score; the vote path falls back to it when the socket is already
    on the leader.  Mirrors the metric-side second_i fallback that
    has always existed.  All subsequent gates (bad_checkpoints,
    util_ok, rate_ok, settle, freeze, explore protection) still apply.

    STATE_VERSION 17 -> 18; state file auto-rejected on mismatch.

 -- cddeppe <91772433+cddeppe@users.noreply.github.com>  Wed, 23 Sep 2026 19:00:00 +0000

bpftune (0.4.69) UNRELEASED; urgency=medium

  * rate: byte-accurate.  0.4.68 computed sustained rate from
    (segs_out + segs_in) * mss_cache / elapsed.  tcp_sock.segs_out
    counts a GSO super-segment as one segment, so the byte total was
    undercounted by the GSO factor.  Measured on heavy 2026-09-23
    against the raw kernel estimate on the same socket at the same
    instant: 64 Mbps from the byte-based computation vs 354 Mbps
    from tp->rate_delivered, a ~5.5x undercount.

    bpf_tcp_sock exposes bytes_acked and bytes_received -- real byte
    counters, retransmit-excluded.  Switch the rolling window to
    bytes_acked + bytes_received.  GSO-agnostic, TLS-agnostic, and
    measures goodput rather than sent-rate.

    conn_state.rate_win_segs is renamed to rate_win_bytes; no
    STATE_VERSION change (sk_storage is ephemeral and the value is
    only read on the initial window seed where 0 is the sentinel).

 -- cddeppe <91772433+cddeppe@users.noreply.github.com>  Wed, 23 Sep 2026 18:30:00 +0000

bpftune (0.4.68) UNRELEASED; urgency=medium

  * sustained-rate correction.  rate_delivered is now computed from
    actual segment movement over a >=500ms wall-clock window, not
    from tp->rate_delivered / tp->rate_interval_us.

    tp->rate_delivered is a burst estimate that updates on ACK
    arrival and can hold a value from minutes ago.  Measured on
    heavy 2026-09-23, comparing the reported rate to actual segment
    movement over the same interval:

      cookie 67545  actual  168 Kbps   reported  9.6-42.8 Mbps   (57-250x high)
      cookie 67483  actual  1.8 Mbps   reported  38 Mbps         (21x high)
      cookie 67463  actual  2.9 KB/s   reported  7.2 Mbps        (2500x high)
      cookie 67437  actual  200 Kbps   reported  7.3 Mbps        (37x high)

    Every downstream consumer reads this value: last_rate_bps, the
    d=1 and d=3 thresholds, rate_ema, swap_score's ratio, and the
    metric's rate term.  The tuner has been acting on numbers
    decoupled from actual throughput -- including on the sockets
    that most need rescuing.

    New: conn_state gains rate_win_ts_ns / rate_win_segs.  The vote
    path seeds the window on the first call, updates every >=500ms.
    Below 500ms it carries last_rate_bps unchanged.  First vote
    reports 0 so no tier acts on a one-shot reading.

    tp->rate_delivered remains in the srate printk for cross-check
    against the log until the new value has been validated.

