bpftune (0.4.66) UNRELEASED; urgency=medium

  * explore protection scales with the explore rate.

    EXPLORE_PROTECT_VOTES=3 protects a fresh socket from being
    swapped for its first three votes so a rare exploration pick can
    prove itself before the swap engine second-guesses it.  That
    assumption inverts when exploration is most of the traffic: at
    --exp=100 every socket is an exploration pick, so the fixed
    3-vote window protects the entire population and the swap engine
    never fires on the traffic it is meant to serve.

    Measured on the heavy host, 2026-09-23 window: 181 home-bucket
    votes, 5 swaps.  75% of votes are cookie-index 0 or 1, i.e.
    inside the protection window; only 2 of 133 structurally
    eligible votes converted.

    Fix: explore_protect_votes() reads tuner_config_map[0] and
    returns 3 at pct<=5 (original behaviour) ramping linearly to 0
    at pct>=100.  The swap-trigger site reads the helper instead of
    the constant.  No struct, map, or STATE_VERSION change.

 -- cddeppe <91772433+cddeppe@users.noreply.github.com>  Wed, 23 Sep 2026 15:00:00 +0000

bpftune (0.4.65) UNRELEASED; urgency=medium

  * swap_score attribution fixes.  Two bugs in the way the score
    learned from swap outcomes:

    1. score_pending_swap fires at the first vote >= T+60 and reads
       the current rate.  If the socket had already rejected the swap
       and moved to another algorithm, that rate belonged to the
       replacement, not the target.  The target was scored on another
       algorithm's performance.
    2. When a new swap replaced an earlier pending one, the earlier
       target's outcome was silently dropped.  That is exactly the
       "socket rejected the target" case, which is the strongest
       negative signal a swap can produce.

    Fix: verify current alg == pending target before applying the
    rate update (otherwise loss-class), and score a pending target as
    a loss the moment a new swap replaces it.  A new helper
    score_pending_rejected() runs from all seven swap sites before
    the state is overwritten.  No struct, map, or STATE_VERSION
    change.

