bpftune (0.4.64) UNRELEASED; urgency=medium

  * origin gate: replace the data_segs_out * 4 < data_segs_in heuristic
    with a port test (remote_port == 443 || 80 -> skip).  The heuristic
    was supposed to catch origin-facing sockets whose rate is set by
    the CDN, not us, but it leaked ~9% of swaps (93 of 1014 on the
    0.4.63 window, heavy host).  Measured same window, direction
    classified from the met rport:
      client-facing  n=482  win 26%  null 70%  loss  5%
      origin-facing  n= 93  win 21%  null 53%  loss 26%
    Origin swaps don't just fail to help; they lose 5x as often.
    A cwnd reset perturbs the ACK pacing the CDN is already adapting
    to.  A port test does not leak.  Heuristic kept below the port
    test as a backstop for origins on non-standard ports.

  * exploration: runtime-tunable percentage for fresh sockets.  New
    BPF_MAP_DEF tuner_config_map (ARRAY, 1 entry, __u32), read at
    ESTABLISHED; the 0.4.22 tight-bucket boost is preserved (at
    pct=100 the boost is a no-op by construction).  The map is
    pinned by the daemon at /sys/fs/bpf/bpftune/tcp_conn/explore.
      bpftune --exp        prints the current value
      bpftune --exp=N      sets it (N = 0..100), live
    --exp=0 holds the picker at the leader (deterministic);
    --exp=100 explores every fresh socket.  No STATE_VERSION bump
    (the explore value is not part of tcp_conn_tuner.state).  The
    CLI records the value to /var/lib/bpftune/explore_pct; the
    daemon restores it on restart.

  * Verifier: ESTABLISHED 45346 insns / 3205 states; vote 19302 /
    1426.  The growth is the map lookup + modulo in the ESTABLISHED
    selection point.  Well under the 1M limit, but the ESTABLISHED
    path is the one the 0.4.40 lesson flagged as verifier-fragile.

