 -- bpftune <builder@example.com>  Thu, 17 Sep 2026 19:57:36 +0200

bpftune (0.4.41) UNRELEASED; urgency=medium

  * Skip origin-facing sockets in the vote path.

    A TCP proxy has two socket directions.  Client-facing (we send
    video to the user) is the one our CC controls.  Origin-facing (we
    receive video from a CDN) is not -- our CC on that socket governs
    HTTP requests and ACKs, not the download rate.  The tuner treated
    both identically; 0.4.39's diagnostic showed 57.7% of 09-16 swaps
    were origin-facing (88% on 09-17 morning).

    Vote path now skips sockets where segs_out * 4 < segs_in, i.e.
    where we are primarily receiving.  Client-facing sockets have
    segs_out >= segs_in and are unaffected.

  * Suppress moderate-tier swaps on a client-facing socket that is
    already in motion (falling or oscillating).

    Falling (metric dropping = delivered rate lifting) is the classic
    shape of an ABR probe: the player requested a higher rendition and
    the socket is climbing to meet it.  Swapping resets cwnd, shows a
    dip to the ABR measurement window, and the higher rendition is
    reverted.  Oscillating (metric bouncing) means a cwnd reset lands
    mid-swing and makes the next swing worse.

    Measured client-facing: falling n=6 win 0% loss 33%; oscillating
    n=17 win 12% loss 24%.  Both below the tier baseline (d=1 ~34%).

    After the origin gate above, every socket reaching the swap
    decision is sending-dominant, so no separate client-side check is
    required for this gate.

  * No struct change.  No state file change.

